  "output": "[{\"body\":\"Found during #572 (Batch 8 shared native exclusion, PR to follow). Parent: #554 / #545.\\n\\n## Problem\\nThe shared seeding domain claim (`seeding_domain_claims`, entered via `seeding.exclusion.enter_domain` from `seeding.cli.run_seed_command`) excludes the native seeding CLI and the dedicated dispatch worker from each other. The legacy container pipeline writes the same `audits` table without taking that claim:\\n\\n- `etl/Dockerfile:35` \u2014 `ENTRYPOINT [\\\"python\\\", \\\"-m\\\", \\\"etl.worker\\\"]`; `docker-compose.prod.yml:161` (and the other compose files) run it as the `etl` service.\\n- `etl/worker.py:46` \u2014 `ETL_RUN_ON_START` defaults to `\\\"true\\\"`; jobs run in fire-and-forget threads (`etl/worker.py:108-112`) under its own advisory key `874321` (`:11`), which is held only by the scheduling loop.\\n- `etl.backfill` \u2192 `KenyaDataPipeline.download_and_process_document` \u2192 `etl/database_loader.py:526-537` inserts `Audit` rows (also `entities`, `fiscal_periods`, `source_documents`, `budget_lines`, `population_data`, `gdp_data`, `economic_indicators`).\\n- It writes no `ingestion_jobs` RUNNING row and takes no `seeding_domain_claims` row, so neither the native CLI nor dispatch can observe it.\\n\\n`etl/scheduler.py` reaches the same loader (no launcher in the repo).\\n\\n## Impact\\nWherever the `etl` service points at the same database as `seed.yml`/dispatch, OAG audit rows can be written concurrently by two pipelines; `source_documents.url` has no unique constraint, so the same OAG document can be registered twice. Dedicated dispatch activation (#554) cannot claim exclusion against all `audits` writers while this runs.\\n\\n## Acceptance\\nDecide and implement one: route the loader's audit writes through the shared claim for `audits` (and the other domains it writes), or retire/quiesce the `etl` service for any database the native/dispatch runners use, with a startup guard that refuses to run against it. Prove with separately launched processes on an owned PostgreSQL fixture that no conflicting second committed effect occurs in either order.\\n\\nInventory with receipts: `batch8-exclusion-evidence/writer-inventory.md` in the #572 PR.\\n\",\"number\":581,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Seeding exclusion gap: legacy etl.worker \u2192 DatabaseLoader writes audits outside the shared domain claim\"},{\"body\":\"Follow-up to #572 / draft PR #584. Parent: #554 / #545.\\n\\n## Current state\\nThe shared domain claim deliberately retains uncertain execution after cancellation, lost continuity/lease, supervisor/child death or malformed receipt. Later runs of the retained domain refuse work until an operator establishes quiescence and reconciles the exact effects.\\n\\nDraft #584 now includes truthful operator-release columns (`released_at`, `reconciled_by`, `reconciliation`) and a written draft procedure. This supersedes the initial report that no truthful schema representation existed. No reviewed runtime/operator reconciliation path has been implemented or proved incapable of releasing a live writer. The PR is held and the production migration has not been accepted.\\n\\n## Acceptance\\nA reviewed audited operator path: evidence that every domain writer/process/session is stopped, exact effects and observations reconciled, distinct who/when/why/evidence recorded without fabricated runner return, no expiry-based reclaim, and executed controls that refuse live/uncertain writer release. A complete transaction must reconcile the claim, the audits dispatch-domain row/command and correlated RUNNING observations. The current CHECK rejects empty/space-only reconciliation text but still accepts tab/newline/Unicode-whitespace-only text; tighten that as part of this follow-up.\\n\\n## Pre-merge / pre-activation gates \u2014 unexecuted\\n1. Actual read-only production `ingestion_jobs` RUNNING census, grouped by domain. Untagged or invalid/mismatched/released claim tags refuse their domain without an age limit. Reconcile them before the first post-migration nightly.\\n2. Evidence for the actual production pooler/direct path and idle-transaction cutoff relative to longest domain runs. The dedicated transaction-scoped lock is tested on owned direct PostgreSQL, not a real production pooler.\\n3. A reviewed operational reconciliation procedure and quiescence across all relevant writers. Legacy ETL (#581), bootstrap (#582) and manual entry points must be accounted for before relying on all-writer exclusion.\\n\\nCoordinator #584 code repair tests pass on owned PostgreSQL, including atomic acknowledgement and minimum/current SQLAlchemy. Those results do not complete these production facts or authorize migration, activation or operator release. Keep #584 draft, #572/#554/#545 open. No production access or operation was used in the review.\\n\",\"number\":583,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Retained seeding domain claims: reviewed reconciliation procedure and pre-merge production gates\"}]\n"
}
